<body>

<header>
  <div class="header-inner">
    <div class="breadcrumb">
      <a href="../../index.html">Steve Zuiker</a>
      <span>›</span>
      <a href="../index.html">Artifacts</a>
      <span>›</span>
      AI Literacy in Applied Contexts
    </div>
    <h1>AI Literacy in Applied Contexts</h1>
    <div class="meta">
      <span>April 22, 2026</span>
      <span>Steve &amp; Claude</span>
      <span><a href="https://claude.ai/chat/84b40731-4963-4889-a2a2-7630cbf9d211">source conversation</a></span>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="featured">
  <div class="featured-inner">
    <div class="featured-label">Latest response</div>
    <div class="featured-prompt">auto shops, dairy farms. Morrisville students are likely to encounter AI as embedded.... is perseveration "blind" trust? It's more subtle than that; more engrained perhaps?</div>
    <div class="featured-response">
      <p>You're right on both. "Blind trust" is too dramatic — perseveration is more habitual, more automatic. It's not that the student chooses to trust without thinking. It's that they don't pause to think at all. The questioning reflex never fires.</p>
      <p>And dairy farms, not precision farms — keep it grounded in Morrisville's actual context.</p>
      <p><strong>Morrisville Keynote: AI Literacy in Applied Contexts</strong></p>
      <p>AI is cognition, not a tool. Students should practice reading, thinking, and writing with AI deliberately and critically, wherever they work: nursing clinics, auto shops, dairy farms. Morrisville students are more likely than students who use AI as a compositional partner to encounter AI as embedded, directive systems. The distinctive pedagogical challenge is perseveration, the cognitive tendency to continue along a path the AI has established, even when conditions warrant reconsideration. Faculty should design repeated practice with AI outputs in realistic contexts where students develop the habit of validating and verifying before acting. Literacy emerges through repeated practice, not instruction.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<main>
  <div class="thread-label">Full thread — reverse chronological</div>

  <!-- Exchange 14 -->
  <details>
    <summary>
      <span class="summary-text">auto shops, dairy farms. Is perseveration "blind" trust?</span>
      <span class="summary-time">9:08 AM</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="response-body">
      <p>You're right on both. "Blind trust" is too dramatic — perseveration is more habitual, more automatic. It's not that the student chooses to trust without thinking. It's that they don't pause to think at all. The questioning reflex never fires.</p>
      <p>And dairy farms, not precision farms — keep it grounded in Morrisville's actual context.</p>
      <p><strong>Morrisville Keynote: AI Literacy in Applied Contexts</strong></p>
      <p>AI is cognition, not a tool. Students should practice reading, thinking, and writing with AI deliberately and critically, wherever they work: nursing clinics, auto shops, dairy farms. Morrisville students are more likely than students who use AI as a compositional partner to encounter AI as embedded, directive systems. The distinctive pedagogical challenge is perseveration, the cognitive tendency to continue along a path the AI has established, even when conditions warrant reconsideration. Faculty should design repeated practice with AI outputs in realistic contexts where students develop the habit of validating and verifying before acting. Literacy emerges through repeated practice, not instruction.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 13 -->
  <details>
    <summary>
      <span class="summary-text">Eight 100-word summary of the talk outline</span>
      <span class="summary-time">9:02 AM</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="response-body">
      <p><strong>Morrisville Keynote: AI Literacy in Applied Contexts</strong></p>
      <p>AI is cognition, not a tool. Students must practice reading, thinking, and writing with AI deliberately and critically — wherever they work: nursing clinics, auto shops, dairy farms. Morrisville students differ from academic writers: they encounter AI as embedded, directive systems, not as compositional partners. The distinctive pedagogical challenge is perseveration — blind trust in AI recommendations in high-stakes applied moments. Faculty must design repeated practice with AI outputs in realistic contexts where students learn to pause, question, and verify before acting. Literacy emerges through habit, not instruction.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 12 -->
  <details>
    <summary>
      <span class="summary-text">My only answer is basically practice</span>
      <span class="summary-time">9:00 AM</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="response-body">
      <p>Practice. So students encounter AI-generated outputs in realistic contexts — diagnostic recommendations, precision agriculture data, equipment instructions — and they practice <strong>pausing, questioning, verifying</strong> before acting. Not once. Repeatedly. Until it becomes habit.</p>
      <p>That's different from "write papers with AI" pedagogy. It's not about showing your thinking in a transcript. It's about building muscle memory around skepticism in the moment of decision.</p>
      <p>So for Morrisville faculty: <strong>Design assignments where students work with AI outputs in simulated or real applied contexts.</strong> Nursing simulation where the AI flags something and the student has to decide whether to act. Farm management exercise where the precision system recommends something and the student has to evaluate against their field conditions. Auto shop where the diagnostic system suggests a repair and the student has to verify.</p>
      <p>The pedagogy is: <strong>repetition under conditions that matter.</strong> Stakes don't have to be real; they have to feel real enough that the student takes the decision seriously.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 11 -->
  <details>
    <summary>
      <span class="summary-text">Talk to me about perseveration</span>
      <span class="summary-time">8:54 AM</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="response-body">
      <p>Perseveration — repetitive, rigid adherence to a pattern even when it's no longer working or appropriate. Once you start down a path, you keep going regardless of feedback.</p>
      <p>In applied contexts: A nurse follows the AI recommendation without questioning whether it fits this specific patient's history. The system said it, so it must be right. Perseveration. A farmer applies the AI-recommended fertilizer rate without considering that this year's weather is completely different from the model's training data. Perseveration. A mechanic replaces the part the diagnostic system flagged without actually checking whether that's what's failing. Perseveration.</p>
      <p>That's the failure mode. And the pedagogical problem at Morrisville is: <strong>How do you teach students NOT to perseverate?</strong> How do you cultivate the active judgment that says "the AI says X, but let me think about whether X is right for THIS situation"?</p>
      <p>That's not fluency training. That's not even literacy in the abstract sense. That's <strong>judgment under uncertainty in real conditions where mistakes matter</strong> — patient safety, crop yield, equipment failure.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 10 -->
  <details>
    <summary>
      <span class="summary-text">Morrisville students are more likely to perseverate than a history PhD</span>
      <span class="summary-time">8:57 AM</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="response-body">
      <p>Exactly. That's the critical reframe for Morrisville.</p>
      <p>A history PhD writing a paper might generate AI text, then critically engage it, revise it, challenge it — active intellectual work visible in the transcript.</p>
      <p>A Morrisville nursing student or mechanic or farmer is more likely to encounter AI as <strong>directive, embedded, seemingly authoritative</strong> — a diagnostic system, a precision recommendation, a repair schematic. The threat isn't "did you write this yourself?" The threat is <strong>perseveration: trusting the system because it's dressed up as expertise, without the active judgment that keeps you safe.</strong></p>
      <p>The talk shifts from "AI is cognition, you must practice it" to "AI is embedded in your students' futures, and they need to know when to question it, not just when to use it."</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 9 -->
  <details>
    <summary>
      <span class="summary-text">RTW in applied contexts: not passive reception but active judgment</span>
      <span class="summary-time">8:51 AM</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="response-body">
      <p>So you're repositioning the entire talk for Morrisville's actual students — not college writers, but people who will encounter AI in <strong>applied, embodied, technical contexts</strong>:</p>
      <p><strong>Auto mechanics</strong>: AI-generated diagnostic instructions. <strong>Precision agriculture</strong>: AI systems recommending fertilizer rates, irrigation schedules. <strong>Nursing</strong>: AI in diagnostic support, medication management. <strong>Tractor repair</strong>: AI-generated schematics. <strong>Horticulture</strong>: AI predictions about growth, disease, harvest timing.</p>
      <p>All of these are RTW in applied contexts. Not essays. But reading (instructions/data), thinking (judgment about application to <em>this</em> situation), and writing (decisions made, actions taken, documentation).</p>
      <p>The tension you identified: by the time these systems are in actual use, they may be 95% reliable. Do you still need active engagement? Or does reliability itself become the problem — students outsource judgment because the system is "good enough"? Maybe the move is: <strong>teach active engagement NOW, while AI is rough and unreliable, so that when polished systems arrive, students have the cognitive habit of thinking critically rather than complying.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 8 -->
  <details>
    <summary>
      <span class="summary-text">SUNY system-wide: technology sector campuses distinctively challenging</span>
      <span class="summary-time">8:58 AM</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="response-body">
      <p>Right. So the framing becomes <strong>system-wide but differentiated by sector and student population</strong>:</p>
      <p>SUNY comprehensive/research campuses: Students writing papers, conducting research. AI literacy means active prompting, critical engagement with generated text, visible thinking in transcripts.</p>
      <p>SUNY technology sector campuses like Morrisville: Students encountering AI as embedded, directive, authoritative systems in applied professional contexts. The distinctive challenge is perseveration in high-stakes moments — clinical, agricultural, mechanical.</p>
      <p>That framing could generalize: when introducing AI literacy across SUNY, the pedagogy has to match the sector. One size doesn't fit all. The RTW framework holds, but its application differs by where and how AI shows up in the student's actual work.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 7 -->
  <details>
    <summary>
      <span class="summary-text">Morrisville: heavily applied — ag, nursing, auto, equine — reframe "when and where"</span>
      <span class="summary-time">8:48 AM</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="response-body">
      <p>Right. So Morrisville is heavy on applied programs — agriculture, horticulture, automotive, nursing, hospitality, equine science, criminal justice. Lots of A.A.S. and associate degrees, B.Tech. programs. Not a traditional liberal arts school with upper-division seminars.</p>
      <p>So your "when and where" claim breaks. They don't have much upper division in the traditional sense. The cognitive work happens in applied, hands-on contexts — auto mechanics labs, nursing clinics, farm management, hospitality operations.</p>
      <p>Reframe: <strong>AI literacy happens wherever disciplinary practice happens</strong> — whether that's upper-division theory or a nursing simulation or an automotive shop. The principle stays (RTW in your actual discipline), but the venue shifts from "upper-level courses" to "wherever real work happens."</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 6 -->
  <details>
    <summary>
      <span class="summary-text">When and where: upper division, wherever disciplinary thinking happens</span>
      <span class="summary-time">8:37 AM</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="response-body">
      <p>Upper division and beyond. Wherever students do disciplinary reading, thinking, and writing. That's where AI literacy lives — integrated into the work itself, not separate.</p>
      <p>That's the bridge to the RTW framework. You're not saying intro gen ed courses can't touch it. You're saying: by upper division, by the time students are doing disciplinary work, they <em>must</em> be doing it with AI literacy embedded — not as add-on, but as the infrastructure of how they read, think, and write in their field.</p>
      <p>"When and where" hooks the faculty to their own courses: <em>Where in YOUR program does real cognitive work happen? That's where AI literacy lives.</em></p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 5 -->
  <details>
    <summary>
      <span class="summary-text">Teaching principle: practice cognition, not use a tool — RTW in your discipline</span>
      <span class="summary-time">8:35 AM</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="response-body">
      <p>The teaching principle: You can't teach students to <em>use</em> cognition as a tool. You teach them to <em>practice</em> cognition — deliberately, critically, with judgment about when and how to delegate, what to trust, what to verify.</p>
      <div class="outline-block">
        <p>When writing arrived, you didn't teach "how to use a pencil." You taught reading and writing as <em>practices</em>.</p>
        <p>With printing, you taught people to navigate print — evaluate sources, trust authorities, build arguments from texts.</p>
        <p>With computing, you taught information literacy — not "how to use a database" but how to think <em>through</em> digital systems.</p>
        <p>With AI: you teach students to think WITH AI. Not fluency (button-pushing). Literacy (cognitive judgment).</p>
      </div>
      <p><strong>Students prompt.</strong> Prompting is writing. Writing is thinking. <strong>Students read</strong> what AI produces — real reading, not passive consumption. <strong>Students think</strong> — they disagree, redirect, verify, extend. They maintain intellectual ownership through their decisions about what to accept, reject, or revise.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 4 -->
  <details>
    <summary>
      <span class="summary-text">AI is active agency vs. pencil: responsiveness in the generative loop</span>
      <span class="summary-time">8:33 AM</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="response-body">
      <p>Exactly. The pencil is an actor. Writing itself — the pencil, the paper, the practice — delegates cognitive work. You don't think the same way in your head that you think on paper.</p>
      <p>The distinction sharpens: The pencil as inert object does nothing. But the <em>practice</em> of writing — pencil + paper + the constraint of linearity + the affordance of revision — that's agentive. It reshapes cognition.</p>
      <p>The AI sitting there does nothing. But the <em>practice</em> of prompting — the dialogue, the back-and-forth, the model's responsiveness, the way it redirects your thinking — that practice reshapes cognition.</p>
      <p>The difference: the pencil doesn't talk back. It doesn't redirect. It doesn't suggest. The AI does. The agency is distributed across the network (Latour), but with AI the network is <strong>dynamic</strong>. The model generates; you respond; the model's next output is shaped by your response; your next thinking is shaped by that output. That's why you can't say "AI is just a tool like a pencil."</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 3 -->
  <details>
    <summary>
      <span class="summary-text">Latour on delegation — AI as agentive participant, not passive instrument</span>
      <span class="summary-time">8:33 AM</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="response-body">
      <p>Latour's work on delegation and inscription is the key move here. The specific citation: <em>We Have Never Been Modern</em> (1993), and more directly, actor-network theory — the idea that nonhumans (technologies, instruments, systems) are <em>actors</em> in networks, not just tools wielded by humans.</p>
      <p>The specific cognitive move: Latour argues we delegate cognitive work to objects. A scientist doesn't just <em>use</em> a microscope — the microscope becomes a partner in perception. It has agency in what gets seen. It shapes the thinking.</p>
      <p>For AI: AI isn't a tool the student wields. It's an actor in the cognitive network. It has agency in the generation. The student doesn't think <em>with</em> AI the way you'd think <em>with</em> a pencil. The AI participates in the thinking itself — it responds, it suggests directions, it constrains possibilities, it opens others.</p>
      <p>Citation: Latour, Bruno. "Where are the missing masses? The sociology of a few mundane artifacts." In <em>Shaping Technology/Building Society</em> (1992). Or chapters on delegation in <em>Pandora's Hope</em> (1999).</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 2 -->
  <details>
    <summary>
      <span class="summary-text">Cognitive augmentation across technologies — AI as first to enter generation itself</span>
      <span class="summary-time">8:31 AM</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="response-body">
      <p>Every technology augments <em>some</em> cognitive function but leaves others unchanged or displaced:</p>
      <div class="outline-block">
        <p><strong>Writing</strong>: augments external memory, displaces oral memorization</p>
        <p><strong>Printing</strong>: augments distribution of thought, displaces scribal labor</p>
        <p><strong>Telegraph/Telephone</strong>: augment transmission (speed), displace proximity</p>
        <p><strong>Radio/Broadcasting</strong>: augment projection of voice, displace local gathering</p>
        <p><strong>Film/Camera</strong>: augment vision (capture/replay), displace live witness</p>
        <p><strong>Computing</strong>: augments storage AND processing (vast amounts of both), but generation remains human</p>
        <p><strong>AI</strong>: First technology that augments <em>generation itself</em> — the production of new thought</p>
      </div>
      <p>That's the cognitive pivot. Every prior tech said "we'll handle X, you focus on Y." AI says "we participate in Y — in the thinking itself."</p>
      <p>Thinkers: McLuhan (media as extensions of sense/cognition), Ong (orality/literacy and consciousness), Vygotsky (thought mediated by cultural tools), Engelbart (augmentation framework), Crawford (material grounding of cognition), Latour (delegation to nonhumans).</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 1 -->
  <details>
    <summary>
      <span class="summary-text">AI is fully transformative — not additive like all prior technologies — computing as partial</span>
      <span class="summary-time">8:27 AM</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="response-body">
      <p>Right. So the corrected claim: writing, printing, telegraph, telephone — those were <strong>additive or marginal</strong>. They extended reach, changed distribution, altered speed. But they didn't fundamentally transform what reading, thinking, and writing <em>are</em>.</p>
      <p>Computing is a partial: it augments storage AND processing in vast amounts. But generation remains human. AI is the first that's <strong>fully transformative</strong> because it participates <em>in the cognition itself</em>. It's not infrastructure around thinking — it's infrastructure <em>within</em> thinking.</p>
      <p>The historical line: writing → printing → telegraph → telephone → computing → <strong>AI (fully transformative, not additive)</strong>.</p>
      <p>The faculty provocation: You're not adopting a new tool. You're teaching reading, thinking, and writing in an infrastructure that has fundamentally changed what those operations <em>are</em>. Your students are already operating there. Your job is to teach them to do it with judgment, deliberately, and well.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 0 -->
  <details>
    <summary>
      <span class="summary-text">Morrisville keynote: 30 min, theory + practice, provocative — AI literacy through the curriculum</span>
      <span class="summary-time">8:23 AM</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="response-body">
      <p>Right. So thirty minutes, theory + practice, deliberately provocative — pushing Morrisville faculty toward <em>collaborative</em> engagement, not tool use or risk management.</p>
      <p>That's a different rhetorical move than Saratoga. Saratoga was positioning (here's what AI literacy IS within gen ed). Morrisville is demanding (here's what you should be doing WITH your students).</p>
      <p>What's the faculty context at Morrisville? What's their starting point — resistance, curiosity, compliance? And what's the specific institutional hook — are they already facing the mandate, or is this a preview?</p>
    </div>
  </details>

