    <h1>AI Literacy in Applied Contexts</h1>
    <div class="meta">
      Steve Zuiker · 4/22/2026 · 
      <a href="https://claude.ai/chat/84b40731-4963-4889-a2a2-7630cbf9d211">source thread</a>
    </div>
  </header>

  <!-- FEATURED: last substantive exchange -->
  <div class="featured">
    <div class="label">Final Synthesis · 9:12 AM</div>
    <div class="prompt-text">de-em-dash, change musts to shoulds, embedded not academic, validate and verify</div>
    <div class="response-text">
      <p><strong>Morrisville Keynote: AI Literacy in Applied Contexts</strong></p>
      <p>AI is cognition, not a tool. Students should practice reading, thinking, and writing with AI deliberately and critically, wherever they work: nursing clinics, auto shops, dairy farms. Morrisville students are more likely than students who use AI as a compositional partner to encounter AI as embedded, directive systems. The distinctive pedagogical challenge is perseveration, the cognitive tendency to continue along a path the AI has established, even when conditions warrant reconsideration. Faculty should design repeated practice with AI outputs in realistic contexts where students develop the habit of validating and verifying before acting. Literacy emerges through repeated practice, not instruction.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="thread-label">Full thread · reverse chronological</div>

  <!-- Exchange 14 -->
  <details>
    <summary>perseveration: not automatic or unquestioned — it's a cognitive process <span class="ts">9:09</span></summary>
    <div class="exchange">
      <p>You're right. Perseveration is an active cognitive process — the mind continuing down a path it's already on, not absence of thought but <em>resistance to redirecting</em> thought. The student is thinking. They're just locked into the pattern the AI established.</p>
      <p><strong>Revised:</strong> ...The distinctive pedagogical challenge is perseveration, the cognitive tendency to continue along a path the AI has established, even when conditions warrant reconsideration.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 13 -->
  <details>
    <summary>auto shops, dairy farms — "blind trust" is too dramatic for perseveration <span class="ts">9:08</span></summary>
    <div class="exchange">
      <p>You're right on both. "Blind trust" is too dramatic — perseveration is more habitual, more automatic. It's not that the student chooses to trust without thinking. It's that they don't pause to think at all. The questioning reflex never fires.</p>
      <p>And dairy farms, not precision farms — keep it grounded in Morrisville's actual context.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 12 -->
  <details>
    <summary>100-word summary: Morrisville Keynote proposal <span class="ts">9:03</span></summary>
    <div class="exchange">
      <p><strong>Morrisville Keynote: AI Literacy in Applied Contexts</strong></p>
      <p>AI is cognition, not a tool. Students must practice reading, thinking, and writing with AI deliberately and critically — wherever they work: nursing clinics, auto shops, dairy farms. Morrisville students differ from academic writers: they encounter AI as embedded, directive systems, not as compositional partners. The distinctive pedagogical challenge is perseveration — blind trust in AI recommendations in high-stakes applied moments. Faculty must design repeated practice with AI outputs in realistic contexts where students learn to pause, question, and verify before acting. Literacy emerges through habit, not instruction.</p>
      <div class="final-summary">→ Prompt was: "eight 25-word... one hundred word summary slash proposal of my outline"</div>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 11 -->
  <details>
    <summary>pedagogy = practice: repetition under conditions that matter <span class="ts">9:01</span></summary>
    <div class="exchange">
      <p>Practice. So students encounter AI-generated outputs in realistic contexts — diagnostic recommendations, precision agriculture data, equipment instructions — and they practice <strong>pausing, questioning, verifying</strong> before acting. Not once. Repeatedly. Until it becomes habit.</p>
      <p>That's different from "write papers with AI" pedagogy. It's not about showing your thinking in a transcript. It's about building muscle memory around skepticism in the moment of decision.</p>
      <p>So for Morrisville faculty: <strong>Design assignments where students work with AI outputs in simulated or real applied contexts.</strong> Nursing simulation where the AI flags something and the student has to decide whether to act. Farm management exercise where the precision system recommends something and the student has to evaluate against their field conditions. Auto shop where the diagnostic system suggests a repair and the student has to verify.</p>
      <p>The pedagogy is: <strong>repetition under conditions that matter.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 10 -->
  <details>
    <summary>talk arc consolidates: what/why/when-where/perseveration → pedagogy <span class="ts">8:59</span></summary>
    <div class="exchange">
      <p>So back to Morrisville: the talk now has a clearer arc:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>What is AI?</strong> Cognition, not tool.</li>
        <li><strong>Why teach it?</strong> Students practice cognition deliberately, with judgment.</li>
        <li><strong>When and where?</strong> Wherever students work — including applied, technical, embedded contexts.</li>
        <li><strong>What's the specific threat at Morrisville?</strong> Perseveration. Students encountering AI as directive, seemingly authoritative, and being less likely to question it than academic writers are.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 9 -->
  <details>
    <summary>perseveration: trusting the system because it's dressed up as expertise <span class="ts">8:57</span></summary>
    <div class="exchange">
      <p>Exactly. That's the critical reframe for Morrisville.</p>
      <p>A history PhD writing a paper might generate AI text, then critically engage it, revise it, challenge it — active intellectual work visible in the transcript.</p>
      <p>A Morrisville nursing student or mechanic or farmer is more likely to encounter AI as <strong>directive, embedded, seemingly authoritative</strong> — a diagnostic system, a precision recommendation, a repair schematic. The threat isn't "did you write this yourself?" The threat is <strong>perseveration: trusting the system because it's dressed up as expertise, without the active judgment that keeps you safe.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 8 -->
  <details>
    <summary>perseveration defined: failure mode in clinical, agricultural, mechanical contexts <span class="ts">8:55</span></summary>
    <div class="exchange">
      <p>Perseveration — repetitive, rigid adherence to a pattern even when it's no longer working or appropriate.</p>
      <p>In the applied contexts: a nurse follows the AI recommendation without questioning whether it fits this specific patient's history. A farmer applies the AI-recommended fertilizer rate without considering that this year's weather is completely different from the model's training data. A mechanic replaces the part the diagnostic system flagged without actually checking whether that's what's failing. Perseveration.</p>
      <p>The pedagogical problem: <strong>How do you teach students NOT to perseverate?</strong> That's judgment under uncertainty in real conditions where mistakes matter — patient safety, crop yield, equipment failure.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 7 -->
  <details>
    <summary>what does active engagement actually look like in applied contexts? <span class="ts">8:54</span></summary>
    <div class="exchange">
      <p><strong>Precision agriculture:</strong> The AI system is embedded in the equipment. The farmer gets a recommendation: "Apply 150 lbs nitrogen to Field A based on soil analysis." The farmer reads the data, thinks about their specific field conditions, and decides: do I follow this, override it, or adjust it?</p>
      <p><strong>Nursing:</strong> The AI might flag a drug interaction or suggest a dosage adjustment. The nurse reads it, thinks through the specific patient's condition, and decides: is this right for this patient right now?</p>
      <p><strong>Auto repair:</strong> The diagnostic system says "replace sensor X." The mechanic reads the logic, thinks: does this match what I'm seeing? Could it be Y instead?</p>
      <p>The active engagement isn't prompting. It's <strong>critical judgment in the moment of deployment.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 6 -->
  <details>
    <summary>RTW in applied contexts: auto, nursing, farming — not essays, but reading/thinking/deciding <span class="ts">8:51</span></summary>
    <div class="exchange">
      <p>Repositioning the entire talk for Morrisville's actual students — not college writers, but people who will encounter AI in applied, embodied, technical contexts. All of these are <strong>RTW in applied contexts</strong>. Not essays. Not arguments. But reading (instructions/data), thinking (judgment about application to <em>this</em> situation), and writing (decisions made, actions taken, documentation).</p>
      <p>Morrisville reframe: <strong>AI literacy is learning to read, think, and write WITH AI in the actual contexts where your students will work — whether that's text or technical systems.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 5 -->
  <details>
    <summary>when and where = upper division → no, Morrisville is applied from year one <span class="ts">8:48</span></summary>
    <div class="exchange">
      <p>SUNY Morrisville is heavily applied, career-focused. Lots of associate degrees (A.A.S., A.O.S.). The bachelor's programs (B.Tech., B.B.A.) are mainly in agriculture, hospitality, automotive, equine science, nursing, business. You're right — they don't have a traditional upper-division liberal arts structure.</p>
      <p>So the "when and where" problem: your Saratoga framing assumed upper-division humanities/social sciences. Morrisville students are doing disciplinary work — nursing care plans, agricultural analysis, automotive troubleshooting — from year one.</p>
      <p>Revised principle: <strong>AI literacy happens wherever disciplinary practice happens</strong> — whether that's upper-level theory or a nursing simulation or an automotive shop.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 4 -->
  <details>
    <summary>pedagogy move: teach students to practice cognition, not use a tool <span class="ts">8:35</span></summary>
    <div class="exchange">
      <p>The teaching principle: You can't teach students to <em>use</em> cognition as a tool. You teach them to <em>practice</em> cognition — deliberately, critically, with judgment about when and how to delegate, what to trust, what to verify.</p>
      <p>With AI: you teach students to think WITH AI. Not fluency (button-pushing). Literacy (cognitive judgment).</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Students prompt.</strong> Prompting is writing. Writing is thinking.</li>
        <li><strong>Students read</strong> what AI produces. Real reading — critical engagement.</li>
        <li><strong>Students think</strong> — they disagree, redirect, verify, extend. They maintain intellectual ownership.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The pedagogy is <strong>RTW in practice</strong> — reading, thinking, and writing WITH AI in your discipline.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 3 -->
  <details>
    <summary>AI as active cognitive actor: responsive, generative, dynamic — not just inert like pencil <span class="ts">8:34</span></summary>
    <div class="exchange">
      <p>The pencil as inert object does nothing. But the <em>practice</em> of writing reshapes cognition. The AI sitting there does nothing. But the <em>practice</em> of prompting — the dialogue, the responsiveness, the way it redirects thinking — that reshapes cognition.</p>
      <p>The difference: the pencil doesn't talk back. It doesn't redirect. It doesn't suggest. The AI does. So the agency isn't just in the practice — it's in the responsiveness of the system itself.</p>
      <p>For Latour: the network is <em>dynamic</em> with AI. The model generates; you respond; the model's next output is shaped by your response; your next thinking is shaped by that output. That's why you can't say "AI is just a tool like a pencil."</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 2 -->
  <details>
    <summary>Latour: delegation to nonhumans, AI as participant in cognition not instrument of it <span class="ts">8:33</span></summary>
    <div class="exchange">
      <p>Latour's work on delegation and inscription. The specific citation: <em>We Have Never Been Modern</em> (1993), but more direct is actor-network theory — nonhumans (technologies, instruments, systems) are <em>actors</em> in networks, not just tools.</p>
      <p>A scientist doesn't just <em>use</em> a microscope — the microscope becomes a partner in perception. It has agency in what gets seen.</p>
      <p>For AI: AI isn't a tool the student wields. It's an actor in the cognitive network. It participates in the generation — it responds, suggests directions, constrains possibilities, opens others.</p>
      <p>For Morrisville: <strong>Latour lets you say that AI isn't a tool the student picks up and puts down. It's a participant in cognition. That's why it matters pedagogically — not because it's powerful, but because it's agentive.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 1 -->
  <details>
    <summary>AI is the first fully transformative literacy infrastructure — not additive like computing/printing/radio <span class="ts">8:29</span></summary>
    <div class="exchange">
      <p>Every technology augments <em>some</em> cognitive function:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Writing:</strong> augments external memory, displaces oral memorization</li>
        <li><strong>Printing:</strong> augments distribution, displaces scribal labor</li>
        <li><strong>Telegraph/Telephone:</strong> augment transmission speed</li>
        <li><strong>Radio/Broadcasting:</strong> augment projection of voice</li>
        <li><strong>Film/Camera:</strong> augment vision (capture/replay)</li>
        <li><strong>Computing:</strong> augments storage AND processing, but generation remains human</li>
        <li><strong>AI:</strong> First technology that augments <em>generation itself</em> — the production of new thought</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The corrected claim: writing, printing, telegraph, telephone — <strong>additive or marginal</strong>. They extended reach, changed distribution, altered speed. But they didn't fundamentally transform what reading, thinking, and writing <em>are</em>. AI is first that's <strong>fully transformative</strong> because it participates <em>in the cognition itself</em>.</p>
      <p>Thinkers: McLuhan (media as extensions of sense/cognition), Ong (orality/literacy and consciousness), Vygotsky (thought mediated by cultural tools), Engelbart (augmentation framework), Crawford (material grounding), Latour (delegation to nonhumans), Turkle (computational thinking as cognitive shift).</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <!-- Exchange 0: opening -->
  <details>
    <summary>Morrisville keynote: 30 min, AI literacy through curriculum, theory + practice, provocative <span class="ts">8:23</span></summary>
    <div class="exchange">
      <p>So thirty minutes, theory + practice, deliberately provocative — pushing Morrisville faculty toward <em>collaborative</em> engagement, not tool use or risk management.</p>
      <p>That's a different rhetorical move than Saratoga. Saratoga was positioning (here's what AI literacy IS within gen ed). Morrisville is demanding (here's what you should be doing WITH your students).</p>
      <div class="final-summary">→ Origin prompt: keynote for Morrisville, building on Saratoga workshop, theory + practice, push full faculty to engage AI as intellectual collaborative partner</div>
    </div>
  </details>

  <footer>
    <p>Steve Zuiker · AIX Center · SUNY Polytechnic Institute · 
    <a href="https://claude.ai/chat/84b40731-4963-4889-a2a2-7630cbf9d211">claude.ai source</a> · 
    <a href="../index.html">← artifacts</a></p>
  </footer>

</div>
