{
  "kind": "email",
  "summary_title": "Email to provost — applied/compositional AI literacy distinction",
  "variants": [
    {
      "subject": "AI literacy in applied contexts",
      "body": "Good to connect the other day.

I've been developing a distinction that seems relevant to institutions like yours. Most AI literacy pedagogy in higher ed has been built for what I'm calling the compositional case — the student prompts a model, gets a text response, and works with that output to write, revise, or argue. The transcript is the evidence of thinking.

Applied contexts are different. A nursing student, a manufacturing technician, an automotive mechanic encounters AI as an embedded, directive system — it recommends an action, not a text. The cognitive demand isn't revision; it's judgment at the moment of deployment. The failure mode isn't poor writing; it's acting on a recommendation that didn't fit the specific situation.

I'm developing this for a keynote at SUNY Morrisville and, I notice, also now for the Manufacturing Workforce Development Forum at Farmingdale in June — different topic, same underlying distinction.

If the applied/compositional frame is useful for your work, I'd be glad to think through it together.

//steve."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "kind": "email",
  "summary_title": "Email to provost Jason \u2014 applied/compositional distinction",
  "variants": [
    {
      "subject": "AI literacy in applied contexts",
      "body": "Hi Jason, thanks for the email.\n\nI've been developing a distinction that seems important to teaching AI literacy. Most AI literacy pedagogy in higher ed has been built for the compositional case \u2014 the student prompts a model, gets a text response, and works with that output to write, revise, or argue. Transcripts are evidence of thinking.\n\nApplied contexts are different. A nursing student, a manufacturing technician, an automotive mechanic encounters AI as an embedded, directive system \u2014 it recommends an action, not a text. The cognitive demand isn't revision; it's judgment at the moment of deployment. The failure mode isn't poor writing; it's a range of cognitive errors that follow from acting on AI recommendations without adequate critical evaluation.\n\nI'm developing this for a keynote at SUNY Morrisville and for the Manufacturing Workforce Development Forum at Farmingdale in June. If it's useful for your work, I'd be glad to think through it together.\n\n//steve."
    }
  ]
}

